                  {item}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <div class="mt-6 pt-6 border-t border-gray-200">
              <a
                href="tel:3027350050"
                class="w-full inline-flex justify-center items-center rounded-full bg-[#A61E22] px-6 py-3 text-white font-bold hover:bg-[#8C181B] transition"
              >
                Call (302) 735-0050 Now
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Service Types Detail -->
  <section id="services" class="bg-[#F8F8F5] py-20">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="text-center mb-14">
        <p class="text-[#9A9D62] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sm mb-3">Types of Broken Key Extraction</p>
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-4">
          Broken Key Extraction Services in Delaware
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          A broken key situation varies depending on where it happened and what type of lock is involved. Here's how we approach each common scenario throughout Delaware.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-8">

        <!-- Residential Door Lock -->
        <div id="door-lock" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 shadow-sm grid md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-white" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M3 9.75L12 3l9 6.75V21a1 1 0 01-1 1H4a1 1 0 01-1-1V9.75z" />
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 22V12h6v10" />
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Broken Key in a Residential Door Lock in Delaware</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Breaking a key in a residential door lock — whether it's a front door deadbolt, a back door knob lock, or a side entry lever — is one of the most common situations we're called to handle. It usually happens when a key is already showing wear, when a lock is running stiff, or when the key is turned too forcefully at an angle. The break typically leaves a fragment lodged in the cylinder with little or nothing to grip from the outside.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Our locksmiths use professional hook picks and extraction tools specifically designed to grip key fragments within a cylinder and work them out without disturbing the pins. In most cases the lock can be preserved and reused after the extraction. We then assess whether the lock is still in good working condition or if the incident revealed a pre-existing problem — a <a href="/services/lock-repair" class="text-[#A61E22] hover:underline font-medium">stiff lock</a> that contributed to the break, for example.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              After extraction, we cut a replacement key on-site so you're not left standing at your own door. We serve all residential properties throughout Delaware and can typically respond quickly to your location.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Car Ignition -->
        <div id="car-ignition" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 shadow-sm grid md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-white" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9 17a2 2 0 11-4 0 2 2 0 014 0zM19 17a2 2 0 11-4 0 2 2 0 014 0z" />
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M13 16V6a1 1 0 00-1-1H4a1 1 0 00-1 1v10l2 2h7l1-2zM13 6h3l3 4v4h-2" />
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Broken Key in a Car Ignition in Delaware</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              A key that breaks off in a car ignition is a particularly frustrating situation — your vehicle is immobilized until the fragment is removed. Car ignition cylinders are more complex and tightly toleranced than most door locks, which means extraction requires specialized tools and a careful approach to avoid damaging the cylinder housing.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              1st Capital Locksmith extracts broken keys from car ignitions throughout Delaware. We come to your vehicle's location — whether that's your driveway, a parking lot, or the side of the road — and work to remove the fragment using professional automotive extraction tools. If the ignition cylinder was damaged during the break or during a prior forced attempt, we assess whether the cylinder can be <a href="/services/ignition-repair" class="text-[#A61E22] hover:underline font-medium">repaired</a> or needs to be replaced.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              After a successful extraction, we can cut a replacement key on-site for most standard vehicle keys. For <a href="/services/key-fob-programming" class="text-[#A61E22] hover:underline font-medium">transponder keys and key fobs that require programming</a>, call us and we'll confirm compatibility before we head to you. We serve drivers throughout Kent County, Delaware.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Car Door Lock -->
        <div id="car-door" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 shadow-sm grid md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-white" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 118 0m-4 8v2m-6 4h12a2 2 0 002-2v-6a2 2 0 00-2-2H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v6a2 2 0 002 2z" />
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Broken Key in a Car Door Lock in Delaware</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              While most modern vehicles rely heavily on key fobs for door access, many cars still have physical key cylinders on the driver's door — and some older vehicles have them on both sides. A key that breaks in a car door lock is a different situation than an ignition break; the door cylinder is generally more accessible and in many cases easier to extract from.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Our locksmiths extract broken keys from car door lock cylinders throughout Delaware. We come to your vehicle's location, assess how deeply the fragment is lodged and the condition of the cylinder, and use the appropriate extraction tools to remove it cleanly. If the door cylinder has been damaged and needs replacement, we can handle that on the same visit in most cases.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Call (302) 735-0050 with your vehicle's year, make, and model and your location. We'll let you know our ETA and confirm what we can do on-site before we arrive.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Padlock & Other Locks -->
        <div id="padlock" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 shadow-sm grid md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-white" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 15v2m-6 4h12a2 2 0 002-2v-6a2 2 0 00-2-2H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v6a2 2 0 002 2zM10 7a2 2 0 114 0v3H10V7z" />
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Broken Key in a Padlock or Other Lock in Delaware</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Broken keys aren't limited to door locks and ignitions. We also extract key fragments from padlocks, mailbox locks, storage unit locks, gate locks, locker locks, and other keyed hardware throughout Delaware. The approach varies by lock type — padlock cylinders differ from door lock cylinders in their internal construction, and some small lock types require micro extraction tools.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              In some cases with padlocks and lower-security hardware, it may be more cost-effective to cut the lock and replace it rather than attempting extraction — particularly if the lock has no significant value beyond its function. We'll give you an honest assessment of the best approach for your specific situation before doing any work.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Whatever type of lock your key broke in, give us a call and describe the situation. We'll let you know if it's something we can handle on-site and what to expect in terms of cost.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Why DIY Is a Bad Idea -->
        <div id="no-diy" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 shadow-sm grid md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-white" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01M10.29 3.86L1.82 18a2 2 0 001.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 001.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 00-3.42 0z" />
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Why You Should Never DIY a Broken Key Extraction</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              We understand the impulse — you've got a key fragment stuck in a lock and you want it out right now. The problem is that every common DIY approach makes the situation worse. Pushing something into the cylinder to try to grab the fragment almost always pushes it deeper. Tweezers at the wrong angle bend the pins. Super glue on a matchstick sounds clever until the glue bonds to the cylinder itself, which turns a simple extraction into a full cylinder replacement.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Even WD-40 — which many people spray into a lock hoping to help — is actually counterproductive. It's not a proper lock lubricant, and the residue it leaves attracts dirt and gums up the mechanism over time. If the lock was already running stiff (which may have contributed to the key break), WD-40 will make that underlying problem worse.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              The best thing you can do after a key breaks is to stop, leave the fragment where it is, and call 1st Capital Locksmith at (302) 735-0050. The sooner we get there, and the less that's been done to complicate the extraction, the better the outcome — and the lower the cost.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- What Causes Keys to Break -->
        <div id="causes" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 shadow-sm grid md:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-6">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22] rounded-full flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-white" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M9.663 17h4.673M12 3v1m6.364 1.636l-.707.707M21 12h-1M4 12H3m3.343-5.657l-.707-.707m2.828 9.9a5 5 0 117.072 0l-.548.547A3.374 3.374 0 0014 18.469V19a2 2 0 11-4 0v-.531c0-.895-.356-1.754-.988-2.386l-.548-.547z" />
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">What Causes Keys to Break in Locks?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Keys don't break in good locks under normal use without reason. Understanding the cause matters because it determines whether just extracting the key is enough or whether there's an underlying issue that will cause problems again if it isn't addressed.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              The most common causes include: a worn or bent key that has developed a weak point over years of use; a key that has been copied too many times from a copy, resulting in a slightly incorrect profile that creates binding stress; a stiff or jammed lock that resists turning and puts abnormal force on the key; a lock that is misaligned with the door, creating lateral pressure; or simply forcing a key that doesn't quite fit.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              After we complete the extraction, we'll assess the condition of both the key fragment and the lock to identify any contributing factors. If the lock needs lubrication, adjustment, or replacement to prevent a repeat, we'll tell you directly and can handle it on the same visit.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Photo Gallery Strip -->
  <section class="bg-white py-14">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
